  "|| Famous for | the oldest complete text of the Four Branches of the Mabinogi",
  "## A book for a lover of learning",
  "The White Book of Rhydderch was written in the middle of the fourteenth century by five scribes, most likely monks of the Cistercian abbey of Strata Florida, where so many of the treasures of Welsh writing were made.[[cite:nlw-white]] It was made for Rhydderch ab Ieuan Llwyd of Parc Rhydderch in Ceredigion, a lawyer and a lover of Welsh learning, whose praises were sung by the poets of his day, Dafydd ap Gwilym among them.",
  "## What it holds",
  "It holds the oldest complete text of the Four Branches of the Mabinogi, and of the other tales gathered as the Mabinogion: Culhwch and Olwen, the Dream of Macsen Wledig, Lludd and Llefelys and the three romances. Beside them are lives of saints, Welsh versions of the tales of Charlemagne, the history of the kings of Britain, religious writing and poetry. It is the twin of the Red Book of Hergest, and the two great books may come from the same lost exemplars.",
  "## Its story",
  "The book stayed in Ceredigion for some time and later came to the house of Rhiwedog, near Bala. By about 1634 it belonged to John Jones of Gellilyfdy, a scholar and tireless copier of manuscripts, and by then it had been divided into two volumes, as it still is: Peniarth 4 holds the tales and Peniarth 5 the rest. From John Jones it passed to Robert Vaughan of Hengwrt, and with his library to Peniarth in 1859. Sir John Williams bought it in 1904 and gave it to the new National Library of Wales. It was rebound in 1940.",
