  "|| Oldest versions | named after Llanstephan 1, Peniarth 44 and Dingestow",
  "## The history of the kings in Welsh",
  "Brut y Brenhinedd is the history of the kings of the Britons in Welsh: Brutus and his sons, Lleon who built Caerllion, Llŷr and his daughters, Beli and Brân, Lludd who walled London, Caswallon who stood against Caesar, Macsen, Gwrtheyrn, Emrys, Uthr and Arthur, and last of all Cadwaladr. Geoffrey of Monmouth wrote it in Latin about 1136, from what he called a very old book in the British tongue.[[cite:geoffrey-evans]] Within a century the Welsh had made it their own again, in their own language, and no book was copied more often in medieval Wales.",
  "## Many translations",
  "It was translated more than once. The scholars who study it name the oldest versions after the manuscripts that keep them: the Llanstephan 1, Peniarth 44 and Dingestow versions. One version sets inside the history the tale of Lludd and Llefelys. Another, from a manuscript in Jesus College, Oxford, was printed in 1801 as Brut Tysilio and is held by Welsh tradition to be the very book Geoffrey turned into Latin.",
  "## Pictures of the kings",
  "Some copies were given pictures. In Peniarth 23, a later medieval copy of the Brut, the scribe drew the kings in the margins, among them Cadwallon of Gwynedd, the great king of the Britons who fought Edwin of Northumbria.",
